(0) One must begin Exam 1A AS SOON AS the course starts.

(1) One must begin every exam other than Exam 1A as soon as the previous on=
e is finished.

(2) One MUST proceed in a SYSTEMATIC MANNER as follows:

(20) Complete (1a).

(21) Fill in the row for (1a) and enter the appropriate value in the expect=
ed grade column.

(22) Enter the same value as the total.

(23) Complete (2a).

(24) Fill in the row for (2a) and enter the appropriate value in the expect=
ed grade column.

(25) Add the expected grades in the expected grade columns and replace the =
previous value of the total with the current value of the total.

(26) Complete (3a)

(27) Fill in the row for (3a) and enter the appropriate value in the expect=
ed grade column.

(28) Add the expected grades in the expected grade columns and replace the =
previous value of the total with the current value of the total.

(29) Repeat this procedure for (1b), (2b), (3b), (1c), (2c), (3c) in precis=
ely this order, (except in those rare cases in which the problems are not i=
ndexed in this manner) finishing as many as one can before 23:59 on Monday =
night.

(30) SUBMIT THE EXAM ON MONDAY NIGHT , 24 HOURS IN ADVANCE OF THE DEADLINE =
WHICH, FOR EXAMS, IS NORMALLY EVERY TUESDAY AT 23:59 HOURS. This guarantees=
 that one will not be late and one will get some score based on the work th=
at one has completed. If some disaster prevents one from doing any further =
work, one will still get some points if the exam is complete in the sense t=
hat it is ready to be graded.

(31) Follow the algorithm on Tuesday and resubmit with updates.; this submi=
ssion should be complete and ready to be graded as well.

(32) Go over the exam on Wednesday to make sure that every straight line-se=
gment is VISIBLY STRAIGHT, that every bracket of every form occurs in pairs=
, that the indexing is correct, that the page-numbering is correct, that th=
e tildes underlying the row-replacements are not missing, that single under=
lines and double underlines are not missing, the top-sheet is filled in pro=
perly and the numbers added up correctly, and that the submission matches t=
he solved exam in format EXACTLY down to the last detail except for the val=
ues of the parameters and the number of steps needed to solve the problems.

(3) If one is unsystematic, haphazard, and disorganised, one is likely to d=
o poorly.

